   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the navigation law, in relation to adding the United States sailing association to those allowed to give boating safety courses",
    "basePrintNo": "S5533",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5533-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§45, 49, 49-a, 79 & 103, Nav L",
    "lawSection": "Navigation Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5533\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the navigation law, in relation to adding the United\nStates sailing association to those allowed to give boating safety\ncourses\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo allow the United States Sailing Association to be a provider of\nboating safety courses and boating safety certificates.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nAmends the Navigation Law to include the United States Boating\nAssociation as an organization qualified to issue boating safety\ncertificates.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nFor over a decade, the United States Sailing Association has taught\nthousands of New Yorkers in their hands-on Safe Powerboat Handling\ncourses and had been approved to issue New York State Boating Safety\nCertificates. However, in 2010 the New York State Office of Parks and\nRecreation reinterpreted the law and determined that the United States\nSailing Association would not be able to issue New York State Boating\nSafety Certificates through their own courses. As a result, those\nholding a certificate from the United States Sailing Association must\nalso complete another State approved boating course before they can\nboat. Moreover, New York has made it easier to receive a Boating\nSafety Certificate in front of a screen without ever having to\nactually touch a boat. The United State Sailing Association teaches\ncourses in a manner that combines classroom teaching with hands-on\ndrills. By allowing the United States Sailing Association to issue\nBoating Safety Certificates, New York State residents will would have\nthe option to take a hands-on course in boating safety.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew Bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
    "multiSponsors": {
