    "lawCode": "Amd §§45, 49, 49-a, 79 & 103, Nav L",
    "lawSection": "Navigation Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5533A\n\nSPONSOR: MARCELLINO\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the navigation law, in relation to\nadding the United States sailing association to those allowed to give\nboating safety courses\n\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the navigation law, in relation to\nadding the United States sailing association to those allowed to give\nboating safety courses\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo allow the United States Sailing Association to be a provider of boat-\ning safety courses and boating safety certificates.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nAmends the Navigation Law to include the United States Boating Associ-\nation as an organization qualified to issue boating safety certificates\nupon completion of the safe powerboat handling and/or basic cruising\npowerboat courses.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nFor over a decade, the United States Sailing Association has taught\nthousands of New Yorkers in their hands-on Safe Powerboat Handling\ncourses and had been approved to issue New York State Boating Safety\nCertificates. However, in 2010 the New York State Office of Parks and\nRecreation reinterpreted the law and determined that the United States\nSailing Association would not be able to issue New York State Boating\nSafety Certificates through their own courses. As a result, those hold-\ning a certificate from the United States Sailing Association must also\ncomplete another State approved boating course before they can boat.\nMoreover, New York has made it easier to receive a Boating Safety\nCertificate in front of a screen without ever having to actually touch a\nboat. The United State Sailing Association teaches courses in a manner\nthat combines classroom teaching with hands-on drills. By allowing the\nUnited States Sailing Association to issue Boating Safety Certificates,\nNew York State residents will would have the option to take a hands-on\ncourse in boating safety.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
